{
    "background_story": "Colter was a seasoned frontiersman and explorer who played a pivotal role in the annexation of the Oregon Territory in the mid-19th century. Born in Virginia, he joined the Lewis and Clark Expedition as a young man, developing a deep understanding of the American West. Years later, Colter found himself at the forefront of the Oregon boundary dispute between the United States and Great Britain. He advocated tirelessly for American expansion, mapping the region and negotiating with Native American tribes. Colter's expertise and persuasive nature helped sway public opinion in favor of annexation. In 1846, the Oregon Treaty was signed, establishing the 49th parallel as the border between the U.S. and British North America. Colter's contributions to the annexation of the Oregon Territory cemented his place in American history as a champion of westward expansion.",
